  "enhanced_information": "The 4-year-old boy presents with periorbital and lower extremity edema, significant proteinuria (4+), and low serum albumin (2.6 g/dL), consistent with nephrotic syndrome. The recent sore throat suggests a possible post-infectious etiology, but the normal serum complement levels indicate minimal change disease, which is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in this age group. Prednisone is the first-line treatment for minimal change disease, making it the most appropriate next step in management. Other options like enalapril and furosemide are not first-line treatments for this condition, and anti-streptolysin O levels are not necessary given the clinical picture. Cyclosporine is typically reserved for steroid-resistant cases. Therefore, the best management choice is prednisone therapy.",
